WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:07.549
When creating any type of 3D mesh that's to be used with Chaos clothing, there are a few

2
00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:15.429
things you need to know in order for the clothing to react better with the simulation

3
00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:17.890
inside of Unreal Engine 5.

4
00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:23.469
The first thing is the difference between a render mesh and a proxy mesh.

5
00:00:23.469 --> 00:00:28.089
The render mesh acts as, well, what is going to be seen by the end user.

6
00:00:28.089 --> 00:00:35.329
This is often going to be very highly detailed, could be double-sided, have non-uniform triangles,

7
00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:39.289
there can be some pinching or something like that in the fabric, and we can see kind of

8
00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:42.090
that for this cape right here taken from Echo.

9
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:47.380
There is some pinching up here where the cape would be attached.

10
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:55.659
Notice that it doesn't have uniform quadding, we have some geometry that is split in order

11
00:00:55.659 --> 00:01:01.859
to give us that kind of look to the cloak that it's attached, the geometry or the cloth

12
00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:05.659
kind of has these ripples in it as regular clothing would.

13
00:01:05.659 --> 00:01:10.620
Now while this looks really good, the problem is that when we go to simulate this, we're

14
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.420
going to run into a lot of issues.

15
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.340
Also this mesh over here is double-sided, and it's got very high detail at the corners.

16
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.019
The problem again with this is that those simulated vertices will come in contact with

17
00:01:22.019 --> 00:01:27.260
one another and they'll penetrate one another, and that's going to cause problems with the

18
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.260
simulation.

19
00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.859
So what we want to actually do is make something called a proxy mesh, and we can see

20
00:01:30.859 --> 00:01:36.159
the proxy mesh right over here on the left-hand side of our mesh.

21
00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:43.299
The proxy mesh is used to put the simulation on, so we actually take our cloth simulation,

22
00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:48.099
run it on the proxy mesh, and then link that proxy mesh to our render mesh.

23
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:54.780
So the render mesh is offset, but it's offset in a way that doesn't allow for that inner

24
00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:00.980
penetration of the simulation particles in our clothing simulation, and what we'll

25
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:06.799
get out of this is this really highly detailed render mesh that looks like it's rendering

26
00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:12.680
like real clothing, but we're not seeing a bunch of artifacting or having a lot of

27
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.099
issues with it.

28
00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:18.659
The other thing that we need to talk about too is creating level of detail meshes or

29
00:02:18.659 --> 00:02:19.659
LODs.

30
00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:23.259
Now we can create these manually inside of our digital content creation, or we can

31
00:02:23.259 --> 00:02:28.900
use Unreal Engine's built-in LOD tools to create, and no matter what type of cloth

32
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:34.460
you are creating, you do need to make a level of detail for it.

33
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:38.099
As it gets further and further away from the camera, we really want to reduce not only

34
00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:43.659
the amount of vertices that are in it, but all of the clothing cloth settings so

35
00:02:43.659 --> 00:02:47.780
that we can really make sure that when it's super far away from the camera, it's not

36
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.900
consuming as many resources because it doesn't need to really be simulating cloth when it's

37
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.300
only 16 pixels on the screen.
